---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="How I Work | Calileus" description="How Calileus designs C++ software, tests it, and helps teams work together." path="/architecture">
  <main>
    <section class="page-intro mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24" aria-labelledby="architecture-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">HOW I WORK</p>
      <h1 id="architecture-heading">I like designs that are easy to explain and change.</h1>
      <p>I start with the problem and its constraints, then work with the team to keep interfaces, tests, and decisions understandable.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section-band mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6" aria-labelledby="capabilities-heading">
      <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">01 / WHAT I DO</p><h2 id="capabilities-heading">The main parts of the work.</h2><p>Clear ownership and small interfaces make it easier for people to work in parallel.</p></div>
      <div class="capability-grid">
        <article class="capability-card"><span class="capability-number">01</span><h3>Useful boundaries</h3><p>I try to give components a clear job and an interface that is straightforward to test and review.</p><div class="tag-row"><span>C++</span><span>Protobuf</span><span>Linux / Windows</span></div></article>
        <article class="capability-card"><span class="capability-number">02</span><h3>Sensor software</h3><p>My automotive work includes processing radar detections and producing tracked-object data.</p><div class="tag-row"><span>Radar</span><span>Automotive</span><span>Simulation</span></div></article>
        <article class="capability-card"><span class="capability-number">03</span><h3>Repeatable checks</h3><p>I help teams make builds, tests, and code review part of the normal development loop.</p><div class="tag-row"><span>CI</span><span>Static analysis</span><span>Testing</span></div></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-band mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6" aria-labelledby="philosophy-heading">
      <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">02 / PRINCIPLES</p><h2 id="philosophy-heading">Clarity helps the next person.</h2><p>Good engineering includes making the reasoning visible, not just getting code to work.</p></div>
      <div class="philosophy-panel"><p>I don't expect an architecture to be right on the first try. I write down tradeoffs, test the riskiest assumptions, and revisit decisions as we learn.</p><ul class="philosophy-list"><li><strong>Clear</strong> — Small parts and explicit decisions.</li><li><strong>Testable</strong> — Components that can be checked on their own.</li><li><strong>Shared</strong> — Working agreements that make it easier for the team to contribute.</li></ul><p>Automotive software has made me especially attentive to how behavior is tested and reviewed.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-band mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6" aria-labelledby="quality-heading">
      <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">03 / CHECKS</p><h2 id="quality-heading">Make routine checks routine.</h2><p>Automation helps catch common problems early and keeps review focused.</p></div>
      <div class="capability-grid"><article class="capability-card"><h3>Builds and tests</h3><p>I have helped introduce automated build and test workflows across components.</p></article><article class="capability-card"><h3>Code checks</h3><p>Formatting and static analysis make the expected standards easier to apply consistently.</p></article><article class="capability-card"><h3>Releases</h3><p>Repeatable versioning and release steps help teams understand what is shipping.</p></article></div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>